# Training data. The source datasets are inputs we don't redistribute, and synthetic_v4.csv is
# regenerated exactly by `python training/synth_data.py --seed 4`, so none of it belongs in git.
res/

# Superseded model bundles. Only the live bundle (models/v8) and the triage filter are tracked;
# v1, v3 and v4 are kept on the machines that need them for rollback or comparison. Their objects
# are still in this repo's LFS history from earlier commits — removing them from tracking does
# not reclaim that storage, which would need a history rewrite.
# v5 was the live bundle until v6 replaced it, and v6 until v8 did. Each is dropped from git
# rather than kept alongside the next: two 371 MB ONNX files in LFS is ~742 MB against a 1 GB
# free tier, and every clone pulls both. They stay on the machines that already have them, so a
# rollback - including the grievance.enabled: true path that settings.yaml documents against
# models/v6 - is a local copy plus one line in settings.yaml, and `git log` still has the commits
# they were tracked in.
flask_api/models/v5/
flask_api/models/v6/
flask_api/models/v1/
flask_api/models/v3/
flask_api/models/v4/
flask_api/models/v4.backup_pre_v5/

# OCR recognition models for scripts RapidOCR doesn't bundle. Third-party weights we don't
# redistribute, fetched by `python scripts/fetch_ocr_langs.py <script>` and verified against
# the SHA256 RapidOCR publishes.
flask_api/models/ocr/

# The image model (app/clip.py). A separate bundle from the text model in models/v5.
#
# The CLIP encoder (335 MB) and its text tower (242 MB) are NOT here. They are a third-party
# download whose licence the publisher does not declare (see the note in scripts/fetch_clip.py),
# and 577 MB in LFS would exhaust a free-tier quota on its own - every colleague's clone then
# fails on the data limit rather than on anything we did. `python scripts/fetch_clip.py` pulls
# them SHA256-pinned to a fixed revision, so everyone gets byte-identical weights.
#
# What BONC actually made IS tracked, all 383 KB of it:
#   prompts.npz     the policy, encoded. Rebuildable from config/visual_policy.yaml, but only
#                   if you have the text tower, so shipping it means a serving host needs
#                   neither that download nor the tokenizer.
#   probe.npz       9 KB, and the one thing here nobody can regenerate: it was fitted on 968
#                   of BONC's own labelled pictures. It carries a third of the `minors` recall.
#   model_meta.json what the bundle is, which encoder it expects, and the policy digest.
#   probe_backup_default.npz
#                   the probe AS IT SHIPPED, and now the only copy of it. The note above says
#                   probe.npz is the one file here nobody can regenerate - and the admin
#                   dashboard retrains it in place, with one of those retrained copies already
#                   committed. image_trainer.py takes this backup only if it is ABSENT, so a
#                   fresh clone without it would adopt whatever probe.npz it found as its
#                   "default" and "restore default" would restore a fine-tuned model, silently
#                   and permanently. Tracking it is what makes the dashboard's default real.
flask_api/models/img_v1/*
!flask_api/models/img_v1/prompts.npz
!flask_api/models/img_v1/probe.npz
!flask_api/models/img_v1/probe_backup_default.npz
!flask_api/models/img_v1/model_meta.json

# Test output directories
flask_api/scripts/test_generated_pdfs/
flask_api/scripts/test_stress_pdfs/
flask_api/scripts/test_50_negatives_gen/
flask_api/scripts/*.json
flask_api/scripts/*.txt

# Image datasets for the visual check (app/clip.py). Third-party photographs under their own
# licences, some of it graphic by design, plus BONC's own listing pictures — inputs we don't
# redistribute, exactly like res/ above. `python flask_api/scripts/eval_clip_images.py` reads
# them from here; images/labels.csv records where each one came from.
images/

# NSFW reference classifier (scripts/fetch_nsfw_reference.py). A second opinion used only by
# scripts/eval_sexual_label.py to measure the `sexual` label's recall - nothing in app/ loads
# it, and the service runs identically without it. Third-party weights we don't redistribute.
flask_api/models/nsfw_ref/

# Round 8 candidate bundle, kept for inspection; not referenced by settings.yaml (v5 is live).
flask_api/models/v6_candidate/

# SigLIP 2 bundles, measured and not adopted (see contracts/moderation_result.md). Kept out of
# git like every other third-party weight; scripts/build_clip_prompts.py --family siglip2 rebuilds.
flask_api/models/img_v2/
flask_api/models/img_v2_softmax/

# The public-surface grievance model (app/grievance.py). A DeBERTa bundle, so another 371 MB
# of LFS next to models/v6's 371 MB - 742 MB against a 1 GB free tier, and every clone would
# pull both. Same reasoning as v5 above.
#
# It is fully reproducible, which is why it is safe to leave out:
#   python training/generate_support_complaints.py
#   python training/train_grievance.py --write-splits D:/AI/bonc-grievance-data
#   python training/train.py --data D:/AI/bonc-grievance-data --out D:/AI/bonc-grievance/ckpt --epochs 2 --max-len 192
#   python training/calibrate_export.py --ckpt D:/AI/bonc-grievance/ckpt --data D:/AI/bonc-grievance-data \
#          --bundle flask_api/models/grievance-v1 --version grievance-deberta-v1 --max-len 192
#
# models/grievance-linear-v1 IS tracked, all 928 KB of it: hashed n-grams over the same data,
# 78% caught against the transformer's 83% on the hand-written set, and it means a fresh clone
# has working cover rather than a disabled check. Point settings.yaml at it, or rebuild above.
flask_api/models/grievance-v1/

# Candidate text bundles under test (models/v6new, v6r2, ...). Same reasoning as the grievance
# bundle: 371 MB each against a 1 GB LFS tier, and a candidate that has not passed
# training/accept_model.py has no business in anyone else's clone. The one that passes gets
# tracked deliberately, under its own name.
flask_api/models/v6new/
flask_api/models/v6r2/

# The bundle models/v6 held before the retrain, kept so the before/after comparison stays
# runnable on this machine. Its LFS object is the same OID that v6 already had, so tracking it
# would cost no storage - but nobody else needs a copy of the superseded model under a second
# name, and a path called "original" in a clone invites exactly the wrong guess about which
# one is live.
flask_api/models/v6_original/

# The workplace-grievance category, added incrementally rather than by a full retrain. The
# checkpoint rebuilds from:
#   python training/generate_workplace_grievances.py --check-collisions
#   python training/train_incremental.py --init D:/AI/bonc-grievance-v5/ckpt #          --new res/workplace_grievances.csv --replay D:/AI/bonc-grievance-data-v5 #          --out D:/AI/bonc-grievance-v7inc/ckpt
#   python training/calibrate_export.py --ckpt D:/AI/bonc-grievance-v7inc/ckpt ... --min-temperature 1.0
# res/ is already ignored above, so the 100k-row CSV is not tracked; the generator is.

# The retrained grievance transformer (371 MB). Same reasoning as models/v6new and the first
# grievance bundle: it rebuilds from training/generate_support_complaints.py ->
# train_grievance.py --write-splits -> train.py -> calibrate_export.py --min-temperature 1.0.
# models/grievance-linear-v4 stays tracked at 928 KB so a fresh clone has working cover.
flask_api/models/grievance-v2/
flask_api/models/grievance-v3/

# The grievance bundle carrying the workplace category as well (371 MB). Built incrementally,
# so its rebuild is cheaper than the others above - 11 min against 36-44:
#   python training/generate_workplace_grievances.py --check-collisions
#   python training/train_incremental.py --init D:/AI/bonc-grievance-v6/ckpt #          --new res/workplace_grievances.csv --replay D:/AI/bonc-grievance-data-v6 #          --out D:/AI/bonc-grievance-v8inc/ckpt --fail-on-leakage
#   python training/train_grievance.py --write-splits D:/AI/bonc-grievance-data-v8
#   python training/calibrate_export.py --ckpt D:/AI/bonc-grievance-v8inc/ckpt #          --data D:/AI/bonc-grievance-data-v8 --bundle flask_api/models/grievance-v4 #          --version grievance-deberta-v7 --max-len 192 --min-temperature 1.0
# The --data dir must be the COMBINED splits (train_grievance.py now reads the workplace CSV):
# calibrating a model that answers for both genres on a supplier-only validation set fits the
# temperature to half its job.
flask_api/models/grievance-v7/

# The single text model now in service (371 MB), and the one text bundle that IS tracked, so a
# fresh clone gets a working moderator instead of a 40-minute rebuild: v6's lineage with the
# grievance genre folded in as unsafe, so one model answers both questions on every surface.
# It is listed here with no pattern on purpose - the recipe below is the bundle's only
# provenance, and deleting it with the ignore rule would lose how it was built:
#   python training/generate_workplace_grievances.py --check-collisions
#   python training/generate_support_complaints.py
#   <build the combined corpus: v6's splits at 35% rehearsal + the two grievance files as
#    unsafe/safe - see settings.yaml model.dir for what the mix is and why>
#   python training/train_incremental.py --init D:/AI/bonc-v6new/ckpt #          --new D:/AI/bonc-data-v8single/train.csv --replay D:/AI/bonc-data-v6r2 #          --replay-frac 0.0 --out D:/AI/bonc-v8single/ckpt --max-len 256 --lr 1.2e-5
#   python training/calibrate_export.py --ckpt D:/AI/bonc-v8single/ckpt #          --data D:/AI/bonc-data-v8single --bundle flask_api/models/v8 #          --version bonc-v8-single --max-len 256 --min-temperature 1.0

# The two-headed experiment (models/v7, [unsafe, grievance]). Kept out of git and out of
# service: 0 of 4 acceptance gates, and it published phishing that models/v6 caught. The
# approach is recorded in training/train_dualhead.py rather than deleted, because the failure
# mode - two heads on one backbone pulling against each other - is worth not rediscovering.
flask_api/models/v7/

# Compiled bytecode. Ten of these were tracked until the video-moderation branch removed
# them; the rule keeps them from coming back.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# Transcription output. pipeline/stages/sink.py defaults --output-dir to ./transcripts, so it
# lands in transcripts/ when the CLI runs from the repo root and flask_api/transcripts/ when the
# service transcribes an upload - 24 tmp*.{json,srt,txt,vtt} files were tracked that way, and 220
# more were sitting untracked waiting to be swept into a commit. They are named after the temp
# file the audio came from, so nothing reads them and no history of them is worth keeping.
#
# sample_test_video.* STAYS tracked: it is the transcript of sample_test_video.mp4, which is in
# git and is the input docs/video_transcription_pipeline.md walks through, so the expected output
# is worth having next to it. Hence transcripts/* rather than transcripts/ - a pattern cannot
# re-include a file whose parent directory is excluded.
transcripts/*
!transcripts/sample_test_video.json
!transcripts/sample_test_video.srt
!transcripts/sample_test_video.txt
!transcripts/sample_test_video.vtt
flask_api/transcripts/
